Sure! A great sorting algorithm to implement is the Selection Sort, which has a fairly straightforward process. Selection Sort works by dividing the input list into two parts: the sublist of items already sorted, which is built up from left to right, and the sublist of items remaining to be sorted.

Here’s the basic idea:

1. Find the minimum value in the unsorted list and swap the smallest value with the value in the first unsorted spot.
2. Continue doing the above, and every time trigger another search the distance of the search will move ahead by an next spot showing which last sublist increases.

Now, I'll provide JavaScript code for a Selection Sort method.

```javascript
function selectionSort(arr) {
    // Loop through the entire array
    for(let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        // Assume the current index i is the minimum
        let lowerIndex = i;
        // Search the sublist starting from the i+1 position
        for(let j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
            // If item in j places is less than lowerIndex pulled item
            if (arr[j] < arr[lowerIndex]) {
                // Reassign lower index to point to j breaking found lower item
                lowerIndex = j;

            }
        }

        // After the whole loop is done all items are put to right locations if/when lowerIndex while pointing smaller numbers in places to swap taking all enclosed style boxes as parameter(mapped objects! it divides long task).
        let minToTemp = arr[lowerIndex];
        arr[lowerIndex] = arr[i];
        arr[i] = minToTemp;
    }
    return arr;
}

// Here's an example usage:
const unsortedArray = [64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90];
console.log(selectionSort(unsortedArray));
```
