Certainly! There are many sorting algorithms, and each has its own strengths and use cases. For simplicity, I'll implement the Bubble Sort algorithm, which is conceptually easy to understand. Bubble Sort works by repeatedly stepping through the list, comparing adjacent elements, and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. The process repeats until the list is sorted.

Here's how Bubble Sort works:

1. Most elements are already in their correct order initially.
2. Larger values "bubble up" to the top of the list during the pass.
3. After each full pass through the list, the next-smallest value will be in its final position.

Java example to demonstrate Bubble Sort:
```java
public class BubbleSort {

    private static void bubbleSort(int[] array) {
        int n = array.length;

        // Number of elements, iterate over all of them.
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            // Last i elements are already in place, so no need to adjust them.
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (array[j] > array[j + 1]) {
                    // Swap elements if they're in the wrong order.
                    int temp = array[j];
                    array[j] = array[j + 1];
                    array[j + 1] = temp; 
                }
            }
        }
    }

